ChIMES CPU vs GPU Benchmark
============================

Overview
--------
NVE run of 512 carbon atoms (Carbon-2.0, 2+3+4B Tersoff) used to:

  1. Verify force consistency between CPU and GPU paths
     (per-atom fx/fy/fz at step 0, compared within 1e-3 kcal/mol/Å)

  2. Measure GPU speedup relative to a single-rank CPU run
     (LAMMPS "Loop time" ratio for the 200-step NVE phase)

The input runs in two phases:
  Phase 1 -- run 0 steps, dump per-atom forces at initial configuration
  Phase 2 -- run 200 NVE steps (timed)


Files
-----
in.lammps              LAMMPS input (same physics as test_suite-NVE)
benchmark.py           Analysis script: forces + thermo + speedup report
run_benchmark.sh       Interactive runner (GPU compute node required)
submit_stampede3.slurm SLURM batch script for Stampede3 GPU partitions


Building executables on Stampede3
----------------------------------
This test requires **both** binaries in `etc/lmp/exe/`:

  exe/lmp_mpi_chimes       (CPU)
  exe/lmp_mpi_chimes_gpu   (GPU)

They use different filenames and can coexist.


Step 1: Build the CPU binary (login node)
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

    cd <repo>/etc/lmp
    module purge
    module load intel/24.0 impi/21.11
    ./install.sh

Produces: `exe/lmp_mpi_chimes`


Step 2: Build the GPU binary (login node)
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

First identify your GPU's SM architecture (run on a GPU node, or check
partition docs):

    nvidia-smi --query-gpu=name,compute_cap --format=csv,noheader

On Stampede3 `rtx-small` (Blackwell, compute_cap 12.0), use `CUDA_ARCH=120`.
On `h100` partitions, use `CUDA_ARCH=90`.

**Important — module conflict on Stampede3:**
`impi` requires the Intel toolchain; the `cuda` module requires GCC.
Loading `module load intel impi gcc cuda` in one line deactivates `impi`
and breaks the build (`mpicxx: No such file or directory`).

Use `hosttype=UT-TACC` so `install_gpu.sh` loads intel+impi and adds
gcc/cuda to PATH without loading those modules:

    cd <repo>/etc/lmp
    export hosttype=UT-TACC
    ./install_gpu.sh 120        # 120 = Blackwell; use 90 for H100, 80 for A100

Manual equivalent:

    module purge
    module load intel/24.0 impi/21.11
    export PATH=/opt/apps/gcc/13.2.0/bin:/home1/apps/nvidia/Linux_x86_64/25.3/cuda/12.8/bin:$PATH
    export CUDA_PATH=/home1/apps/nvidia/Linux_x86_64/25.3/cuda/12.8
    ./install_gpu.sh 120

Produces: `exe/lmp_mpi_chimes_gpu`

Verify both binaries exist:

    ls -la exe/lmp_mpi_chimes exe/lmp_mpi_chimes_gpu


Running on Stampede3 -- batch submission (recommended)
------------------------------------------------------
Edit `#SBATCH -A` in `submit_stampede3.slurm` if needed, then:

    cd <repo>/etc/lmp/tests/test_suite-GPU_vs_CPU_benchmark
    sbatch submit_stampede3.slurm

Monitor with:  squeue -u $USER
Results in:    current_output_cpu/   (CPU run)
               current_output_gpu/   (GPU run)
               benchmark_results.txt (summary)


Running on Stampede3 -- interactive session
--------------------------------------------
    idev -p rtx-small -N 1 -n 1 -t 00:30:00 -A <YOUR_ALLOCATION>

    # Runtime modules (sequential load works on GPU nodes):
    module purge
    module load intel/24.0
    module load impi/21.11
    module load gcc/13.2.0
    module load cuda/12.8
    module load python/3.12.11

    cd <repo>/etc/lmp/tests/test_suite-GPU_vs_CPU_benchmark
    ./run_benchmark.sh


Reference results (Stampede3, rtx-small, Blackwell SM 120)
-----------------------------------------------------------
Validated 2026-06-17 on NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell Server Edition
(compute capability 12.0), 512 C atoms, 200 NVE steps, tolerance 1e-3.

Force consistency (step 0, 512 atoms):
  fx: max|Δ| = 0.0       mean|Δ| = 0.0
  fy: max|Δ| = 1.0e-10   mean|Δ| = 1.95e-13
  fz: max|Δ| = 1.0e-10   mean|Δ| = 1.95e-13
  Force check: PASS

Thermo consistency (12 output steps): PASS

Wall-time speedup (200-step NVE loop):
  CPU loop time : 149.1 s
  GPU loop time :  16.3 s
  Speedup       :  9.15x

  OVERALL: PASS


Expected output format (benchmark_results.txt)
-----------------------------------------------
======================================================================
  ChIMES CPU vs GPU Benchmark
======================================================================
...
[1] Per-atom force consistency at step 0
----------------------------------------------------------------------
  Atoms compared : 512
  fx: max|Δ| = ...   mean|Δ| = ...   (tol=1.00e-03)
  ...
  Force check: PASS

[2] Thermo consistency across all steps
----------------------------------------------------------------------
  Thermo check: PASS

[3] Wall-time speedup
----------------------------------------------------------------------
  CPU loop time :  XXX.XXXX s
  GPU loop time :  XX.XXXX s
  Speedup (CPU/GPU): X.XXx

======================================================================
  OVERALL: PASS -- CPU and GPU results are consistent within tolerance
======================================================================


Tolerance rationale
--------------------
GPU atomicAdd reductions are non-associative; per-atom forces can differ
from CPU by a few ULP in double precision.  The 1e-3 tolerance provides
a safe margin while still catching genuine physics errors.  In practice,
forces at step 0 often match to ~1e-10 or better when the same geometry
is evaluated on both paths.


Speedup notes
--------------
For this 512-atom test on Stampede3 Blackwell GPUs, ~9x speedup was
observed for the force-evaluation loop.  Speedup depends on GPU model,
SM architecture match at build time, and system size; larger simulations
typically benefit more from GPU acceleration.
